<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">Ly1JJGFic0clKnByb3RlY3RlZEc2IyZJInlHNiI2IywmSSJpR0YpIiIiRi1GLS1GJDYjJkYoNiNGLA==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L7246" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">That is, the absolute value of the numerical solution is conserved as the simulation procedes. This replicates a very important property of the exact solution <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, namely <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
</Section>
<Section collapsed="false" isCollapsible="true" drawButton="true" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title><Text-field style="Heading 1" layout="Heading 1">Stability for nonlinear problems</Text-field></Title>
<Group labelreference="L7265" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">We conclude by looking at the case of general choices of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>; that is, we allow for the possibility that <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> is a nonlinear ODE for <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> It turns out that the conclusions of the stability analysis are applicable if we restrict ourselves to regions where the true solution is slowly varying. We first demonstrate how this works somewhat heuristically. For general <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, we are trying to solve the following ODE.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L7267" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">f := 'f':
ode := diff(y(x), x)-f(x, y(x));</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LV9JLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nRzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiSSxtcHJpbnRzbGFzaEdGKDYkNyM+SSRvZGVHRigsJi1JJWRpZmZHRiY2JC1JInlHRig2I0kieEdGKEY1IiIiLUkiZkdGKDYkRjVGMiEiIjcjRi4=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L7257" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Let us assume that for <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JlEieEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUrZXhlY3V0YWJsZUdRJmZhbHNlRicvJSxtYXRodmFyaWFudEdRJ2l0YWxpY0YnRjIvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYn">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JlEieEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUrZXhlY3V0YWJsZUdRJmZhbHNlRicvJSxtYXRodmFyaWFudEdRJ2l0YWxpY0YnRjIvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYn</Equation> in some interval, the true solution is approximately constant <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> We can then represent <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYmLUkjbWlHRiQ2JlEieUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUrZXhlY3V0YWJsZUdRJmZhbHNlRicvJSxtYXRodmFyaWFudEdRJ2l0YWxpY0YnLUkobWZlbmNlZEdGJDYlLUYjNiUtRiw2JlEieEYnRi9GMkY1RjIvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYnRjJGQEYyRkA=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYmLUkjbWlHRiQ2JlEieUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUrZXhlY3V0YWJsZUdRJmZhbHNlRicvJSxtYXRodmFyaWFudEdRJ2l0YWxpY0YnLUkobWZlbmNlZEdGJDYlLUYjNiUtRiw2JlEieEYnRi9GMkY1RjIvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYnRjJGQEYyRkA=</Equation> as a perturbative series.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L7268" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">perturbation := y(x) = y[0]+add(epsilon^(j)*v[j](x), j = 1..3);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LV9JLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nRzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiSSxtcHJpbnRzbGFzaEdGKDYkNyM+SS1wZXJ0dXJiYXRpb25HRigvLUkieUdGKDYjSSJ4R0YoLComRjA2IyIiISIiIiomSShlcHNpbG9uR0YoRjctJkkidkdGKDYjRjdGMUY3RjcqJkY5IiIjLSZGPDYjRj9GMUY3RjcqJkY5IiIkLSZGPDYjRkRGMUY3Rjc3I0Yu</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L7281" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Here, <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> is a perturbative expansion parameter. Plugging this into the ODE and retaining the first couple of terms in the series yields an ODE for the first order perturbation.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L7269" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">first_order := convert(series(subs(perturbation, ode), epsilon, 2), polynom);
first_order := isolate(convert(first_order, D), D(v[1])(x));</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LV9JLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nRzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiSSxtcHJpbnRzbGFzaEdGKDYkNyM+SSxmaXJzdF9vcmRlckdGKCwmLUkiZkdGKDYkSSJ4R0YoJkkieUdGKDYjIiIhISIiKiYsJi1JJWRpZmZHRiY2JC0mSSJ2R0YoNiMiIiI2I0YyRjJGQSomSSdsYW1iZGFHRihGQUY9RkFGN0ZBSShlcHNpbG9uR0YoRkFGQTcjRi4=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LV9JLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nRzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiSSxtcHJpbnRzbGFzaEdGKDYkNyM+SSxmaXJzdF9vcmRlckdGKC8tLUkiREdGJTYjJkkidkdGKDYjIiIiNiNJInhHRigsJiomLUkiZkdGKDYkRjgmSSJ5R0YoNiMiIiFGNkkoZXBzaWxvbkdGKCEiIkY2KiZJJ2xhbWJkYUdGKEY2LUYzRjdGNkY2NyNGLg==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L7284" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">We now make the further approximation that both <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JlEiZkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUrZXhlY3V0YWJsZUdRJmZhbHNlRicvJSxtYXRodmFyaWFudEdRJ2l0YWxpY0YnRjIvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYn">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JlEiZkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUrZXhlY3V0YWJsZUdRJmZhbHNlRicvJSxtYXRodmFyaWFudEdRJ2l0YWxpY0YnRjIvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYn</Equation> and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> are constant in the interval of interest:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L7251" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">approximations := [f(x,y[0]) = mu*epsilon, D[2](f)(x, y[0]) = lambda];</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LV9JLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nRzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiSSxtcHJpbnRzbGFzaEdGKDYkNyM+SS9hcHByb3hpbWF0aW9uc0dGKDckLy1JImZHRig2JEkieEdGKCZJInlHRig2IyIiISomSSNtdUdGKCIiIkkoZXBzaWxvbkdGKEY6L0knbGFtYmRhR0YoRj03I0Yu</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L7285" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Putting these into the equation of motion for <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> yields</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L7252" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">first_order := subs(approximations, first_order);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LV9JLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nRzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiSSxtcHJpbnRzbGFzaEdGKDYkNyM+SSxmaXJzdF9vcmRlckdGKC8tLUkiREdGJTYjJkkidkdGKDYjIiIiNiNJInhHRigsJkkjbXVHRihGNiomSSdsYW1iZGFHRihGNi1GM0Y3RjZGNjcjRi4=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L7286" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Finally, let us change variables to <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L7255" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">first_order := isolate(expand(subs(v[1](x) = V[1](x)-mu/lambda, convert(first_order, diff))), diff(V[1](x), x));</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LV9JLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nRzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiSSxtcHJpbnRzbGFzaEdGKDYkNyM+SSxmaXJzdF9vcmRlckdGKC8tSSVkaWZmR0YmNiQtJkkiVkdGKDYjIiIiNiNJInhHRihGOComSSdsYW1iZGFHRihGNkYyRjY3I0Yu</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L7287" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The claim is now that solving <Label label="L7267" view="label"></Label> numerically over a region where <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JlEiZkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUrZXhlY3V0YWJsZUdRJmZhbHNlRicvJSxtYXRodmFyaWFudEdRJ2l0YWxpY0YnRjIvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYn">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JlEiZkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUrZXhlY3V0YWJsZUdRJmZhbHNlRicvJSxtYXRodmFyaWFudEdRJ2l0YWxpY0YnRjIvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYn</Equation> and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> are slowly varying is approximately equivalent to solving <Label label="L7255" view="label"></Label>; i.e., the numeric stencil is really solving a perturbative problem to lowest order. However, the latter is entirely equivalent to the test problem, which means that the stability results of the previous section should carry over to the nonlinear case provided that our approximations are valid. It is easy enough to see that if the stepsize is sufficiently small compared to the scales over which <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYmLUkjbWlHRiQ2JlEieUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUrZXhlY3V0YWJsZUdRJmZhbHNlRicvJSxtYXRodmFyaWFudEdRJ2l0YWxpY0YnLUkobWZlbmNlZEdGJDYlLUYjNiUtRiw2JlEieEYnRi9GMkY1RjIvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYnRjJGQEYyRkA=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYmLUkjbWlHRiQ2JlEieUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUrZXhlY3V0YWJsZUdRJmZhbHNlRicvJSxtYXRodmFyaWFudEdRJ2l0YWxpY0YnLUkobWZlbmNlZEdGJDYlLUYjNiUtRiw2JlEieEYnRi9GMkY1RjIvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYnRjJGQEYyRkA=</Equation> and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> vary, the &quot;slowly varying&quot; condition will be satisfied. Now, it may not be immediately clear to the reader that solving <Label label="L7255" view="label"></Label> is indeed eqivalent to solving <Label label="L7267" view="label"></Label> from a numerical point of view. It is plausible that if the true solution is not much different than a constant, the numerical algorithm is indeed finding the first order perturbation, but is that what is really going on? To answer this, we repeat some of the calculations of the previous section without the assumption <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> As before, we will define the error <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYmLUklbXN1YkdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JlEiRUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUrZXhlY3V0YWJsZUdRJmZhbHNlRicvJSxtYXRodmFyaWFudEdRJ2l0YWxpY0YnLUYjNiYtRi82JlEiaUYnRjJGNUY4RjJGNUY4LyUvc3Vic2NyaXB0c2hpZnRHUSIwRictRi82I1EhRidGNS9GOVEnbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYmLUklbXN1YkdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JlEiRUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUrZXhlY3V0YWJsZUdRJmZhbHNlRicvJSxtYXRodmFyaWFudEdRJ2l0YWxpY0YnLUYjNiYtRi82JlEiaUYnRjJGNUY4RjJGNUY4LyUvc3Vic2NyaXB0c2hpZnRHUSIwRictRi82I1EhRidGNS9GOVEnbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> as the difference between numeric and actual solutions at the <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUklbXN1cEdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JlEiaUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUrZXhlY3V0YWJsZUdRJmZhbHNlRicvJSxtYXRodmFyaWFudEdRJ2l0YWxpY0YnLUYjNiYtRi82I1EhRidGMkY1RjgvJTFzdXBlcnNjcmlwdHNoaWZ0R1EiMEYnRjUvRjlRJ25vcm1hbEYn">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUklbXN1cEdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JlEiaUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUrZXhlY3V0YWJsZUdRJmZhbHNlRicvJSxtYXRodmFyaWFudEdRJ2l0YWxpY0YnLUYjNiYtRi82I1EhRidGMkY1RjgvJTFzdXBlcnNjcmlwdHNoaWZ0R1EiMEYnRjUvRjlRJ25vcm1hbEYn</Equation>th node.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L7273" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">E_def := epsilon*E[i] = y[i]-y(x[i]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LV9JLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nRzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiSSxtcHJpbnRzbGFzaEdGKDYkNyM+SSZFX2RlZkdGKC8qJkkoZXBzaWxvbkdGKCIiIiZJIkVHRig2I0kiaUdGKEYxLCYmSSJ5R0YoRjRGMS1GODYjJkkieEdGKEY0ISIiNyNGLg==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L7274" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">This is actually a little different from what we had before [c.f. <Label label="L7261" view="label"></Label>] because of the <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JlEnJiM5NDk7RicvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUrZXhlY3V0YWJsZUdGMS8lLG1hdGh2YXJpYW50R1Enbm9ybWFsRidGMkY0">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JlEnJiM5NDk7RicvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUrZXhlY3V0YWJsZUdGMS8lLG1hdGh2YXJpYW50R1Enbm9ybWFsRidGMkY0</Equation> factor: this is included for book-keeping purposes, and we can later set it equal to unity. We now derive the equations of motions for the errors as before by subtracting the elements of <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">_Stencil</Font> from <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">Stencil</Font>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L7263" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">Subs := [y(x+h) = y(x[i+1]), y(x) = y(x[i]), y_new = y[i+1], y_old = y[i], x_new = x[i+1], x_old = x[i], isolate(E_def, y[i]), subs(i = i+1, isolate(E_def, y[i]))]:
for j from 1 to 5 do:
    EOM[j] := collect(simplify(subs(Subs$2, Stencil[j]-subs(x = x[i], x[i]+h = x[i+1], _Stencil[j]))), [E[i+1], E[i]])):od;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L7275" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">In these expressions, the error terms are sometimes buried within the arguments of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> For example, in the first EOM (representing forward Euler), there is a term like <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>. To bring these out, we can expand to linear order in <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEnJiM5NDk7RicvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJy8lK2V4ZWN1dGFibGVHRjFGMg==">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEnJiM5NDk7RicvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJy8lK2V4ZWN1dGFibGVHRjFGMg==</Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L7289" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">for j from 1 to 5 do:
    EOM_linear[j] := series((lhs-rhs)(EOM[j]), epsilon, 2):
od;</Text-field>
</Input>
